  <element>
    <id>UMLNote</id>
    <coordinates><x>20</x><y>620</y><w>700</w><h>190</h></coordinates>
    <panel_attributes>Bounded-context integration patterns (spec-01 s1.5; L5). Sales -&gt; Inventory: Customer-Supplier over
item-sold. Pricing -&gt; Sales: Published Language, the event records in the contracts module. Inventory,
Sales, Pricing -&gt; Insight: Conformist; Insight projects the upstream events into its own views. Agent
-&gt; Pricing: Anti-Corruption Layer; markdown-recommended is an untrusted proposal and MarginFloorPolicy
decides, by the same code path as a supervisor's POST /api/markdown-plans (FR11, R5, R10). No shared
kernel: Money, Sku and Quantity are declared per context.
bg=#FFFBE6</panel_attributes>
    <additional_attributes/>
  </element>
  <element>
    <id>UMLNote</id>
    <coordinates><x>740</x><y>620</y><w>750</w><h>190</h></coordinates>
    <panel_attributes>Communication rules (spec-01 s1.3). Asynchronous messaging over Kafka is the primary and, with one exception,
the only inter-service mechanism. Each service owns one H2 database; no table is read across a service
boundary. Synchronous REST is used by external clients and the simulator, and by exactly one service-to-
service call: the agent's read of a batch (a fact with a 404 path, no write). sales-service never calls
pricing-service; it prices from a local snapshot fed by price-rule-published and markdown-applied (ADR 0001).
The LLM is reached only from insight-service behind the MarkdownDecisionAgent and BriefingAgent ports; every
test runs against a stub (ADR 0004). The agents propose or summarise; they never write a price.
bg=#FFFBE6</panel_attributes>
    <additional_attributes/>
  </element>
</diagram>
